@* Ported from Admin/DatabaseBackup.ascx + .ascx.cs + .ascx.designer.cs - the database-backup
   console: name a backup, run it, watch pg_dump's output stream in, and browse the backups
   already on disk.

   ⚠ AccessLevel.AdminOnly IS PRESERVED. MixERPAuthorization.CheckAccessLevelAsync enforces it
   and logs "Access to {Control} is denied to {User} from {IP}." on refusal. Note that it is
   NOT the only gate on the actual backup: Hubs/DbHub.IsValidRequestAsync independently
   requires globalLoginId > 0 AND IsAdmin AND Elevated on the hub connection, so this screen
   being reachable does not by itself permit a dump. Both gates are preserved.

   STRUCTURE. Everything was built procedurally into <asp:PlaceHolder ID="Placeholder1"> by
   CreateHeader -> CreateFormSegment -> CreateGridModal, and it is markup now in that exact
   order, with the ids, classes, inline styles and child order the control tree rendered:

     h1                                              (Titles.BackupDatabase)
     div.ui.form.segment                             (HtmlControlHelper.GetFormSegment())
       > div.inline.fields                           (HtmlControlHelper.GetInlineFields())
           > div.field > label[for=BackupNameInputText] + input#BackupNameInputText
           > input#BackupButton.ui.red.button.disabled.loading
           > input.ui.green.button[onclick='$("#BackupModal").modal("show");']
       > h2.ui.blue.header.initially.hidden          (Titles.BackupConsole)
       > div#Messages[style="overflow:auto;"] > div.ui.celled.list
     div#BackupModal.ui.large.modal
       > i.close.icon
       > div.header > i.database.icon + the title text
       > div.content > the backups table
       > div.actions > div.ui.black.button

   EVERY ONE OF THOSE IS A JAVASCRIPT CONTRACT, which is why none is simplified:
     * #BackupButton STARTS WITH "disabled loading" ON PURPOSE. js/admin/DatabaseBackup.ascx.js's
       connectionListener() is what strips those two classes AND attaches the click handler, so
       the button is inert until the SignalR connection is up. That is the screen's whole
       readiness signal. The click handler also stays the script's - NOT an @@onclick - because
       it reads the backup name out of the DOM, re-adds the classes and invokes the hub.
     * the VIEW BACKUPS button keeps its `onclick` as an HTML ATTRIBUTE (not @@onclick): it is a
       pure client-side Semantic-UI modal call, there is no server handler on it, and so there
       is no RZ10008 conflict.
     * #Messages and `.ui.celled.list` are where AddItem() appends every pg_dump line, and
       InitializeMessage() sizes #Messages and adds "ui segment" to it; the h2's
       "initially hidden" class is removed by the click handler when a run starts.
     * `.ui.blue.header` is how the script finds that h2 - so the class list is byte-identical.

   ⚠ THE BACKUP-NAME DEFAULT IS SERVER-COMPUTED AND IS PRESERVED EXACTLY, including its
   condition: `server.Validate()` ran first and the name was filled ONLY when
   `server.IsValid` - so on a misconfigured DbServer.config the box is EMPTY, which is the
   screen's way of showing that the backup cannot work. The format is
   "{DatabaseName}.{OfficeId}.{UserName}.{DateTime.Now.ToFileTime()}" in the INVARIANT culture.
   `@@bind` is used so a name the user edits is still the one the script reads; the script reads
   the element itself, so both work.

   ⚠ Server.MapPath / ResolveUrl REPLACED. BindGridView() resolved the backup directory with
   System.Web.Hosting.HostingEnvironment.MapPath and built each download link with
   this.ResolveUrl(...). Those became MixERPConfiguration.MapPath (the documented content-root
   equivalent) and PageUtility.ResolveUrl. The DatabaseBackupDirectory value itself still comes
   from Resource/Configs/DbServer.config through ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter, so
   the directory is unchanged.

   ⚠ THE GRID RELIES ON HtmlEncode = false. `new MixERPGridView(false)` (htmlEncode: false) is
   what let the Name column render as a real ANCHOR - BindGridView() puts
   "<a href='...' target='_blank'>name</a>" INTO the Name string. The migrated MixERPGridView
   exposes that constructor argument as HtmlEncode="false", which is set here; without it the
   download links would render as visible markup text and be unclickable. `CssClass += "relaxed"`
   is preserved too, and the component's own Initialize() port prepends "ui striped table ", so
   the class list is still "ui striped table relaxed".

   ⚠ Grid_RowDataBound LOCALIZED EVERY HEADER - `0 .. Count` (all four, unlike MenuAccess's
   0..Count-2). Reproduced with four explicit columns carrying Titles.Get(...) headers, in the
   BackupFile PROPERTY DECLARATION order, which is the order a GridView auto-generated columns
   in and therefore the order the user saw: CreatedOn, LastAccessedOn, LastWrittenOn, Name.
   ⚠ That is NOT the order the loop ASSIGNS them in (Name first), and it puts the clickable
   download link LAST - an easy thing to "tidy" and get wrong, so it is pinned here and the
   nested BackupFile class keeps its original member order for the same reason.

   The catalog comes from ICurrentLogin: AppUsers.GetCurrentUserDB() is the DATABASE NAME being
   backed up and AppUsers.GetCurrent().View is the office/user stamped into the default backup
   name - both return an EMPTY login inside a circuit, which would have produced a backup name
   with no database in it and a PostgreSQLServer that fails validation.

   The whole #region IDisposable of the original - which detached RowDataBound and disposed the
   grid - has no counterpart: that was a server control in a control tree, and there is no tree.

   The original declared no OverridePath, MasterPageId, RemoveTheme or IsLandingPage override
   and implemented no ITransaction. *@

@inherits MixERP.Net.Framework.Controls.MixERPUserControl
@using System.Collections.ObjectModel
@using System.Globalization
@using System.IO
@using MixERP.Net.Common
@using MixERP.Net.Common.Helpers
@using MixERP.Net.Common.Models
@using MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.BackOffice.Internal
@using MixERP.Net.WebControls.Common
@using MixERP.Net.i18n.Resources
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<h1>@Titles.BackupDatabase</h1>

<div class="ui form segment">
    <div class="inline fields">
        <div class="field">
            <label for="BackupNameInputText">@Titles.EnterBackupName</label>
            <input type="text" id="BackupNameInputText" @bind="this.backupName" />
        </div>
        @* "disabled loading" is deliberate - connectionListener() in the controller script
           removes both classes and attaches the click handler once the hub is connected. *@
        <input type="button" id="BackupButton" class="ui red button disabled loading" value="@Titles.BackupNow" />
        @* A pure client-side modal call with no server handler, so it stays an HTML attribute. *@
        <input type="button" class="ui green button" onclick="$(&quot;#BackupModal&quot;).modal(&quot;show&quot;);" value="@Titles.ViewBackups" />
    </div>
    <h2 class="ui blue header initially hidden">@Titles.BackupConsole</h2>
    <div style="overflow:auto;" id="Messages">
        <div class="ui celled list"></div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="ui large modal" id="BackupModal">
    <i class="close icon"></i>
    <div class="header">
        @* BASELINE PARITY: the original CreateModalHeader added an <i class="database icon"> to
           this header and THEN assigned header.InnerText = Titles.DatabaseBackups. The
           HtmlGenericControl.InnerText setter CLEARS Controls, so the icon was destroyed and
           never reached the browser. The icon is therefore deliberately NOT rendered here - the
           header is the title text alone, exactly as the legacy screen showed it. (This is the
           only occurrence of the pattern in all 194 ported controls; it is recorded in the
           oddity list of Modules/migration-summary.md.) *@
        @Titles.DatabaseBackups
    </div>
    <div class="content">
        <MixERPGridView CssClass="relaxed"
                        HtmlEncode="false"
                        AutoGenerateColumns="false"
                        Columns="@this.columns"
                        DataSource="@this.files" />
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
        <div class="ui black button">@Titles.Close</div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private const string ControllerScript =
        "_content/MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.BackOffice/js/admin/DatabaseBackup.ascx.js";

    /// <summary>
    ///     The legacy <c>public override AccessLevel AccessLevel</c>, carried across unchanged.
    ///     <para>
    ///         <c>MixERPUserControlBase.AccessLevel</c> is an ABSTRACT GET-ONLY property, so it is
    ///         overridden rather than assigned - which is also what the probe needs:
    ///         <c>ModuleHost.razor</c> reads the surface off an <c>Activator.CreateInstance</c>
    ///         instance on which no lifecycle method runs, and a getter answers there.
    ///     </para>
    /// </summary>
    public override AccessLevel AccessLevel
    {
        get { return AccessLevel.AdminOnly; }
    }

    private string backupName = string.Empty;
    private Collection<BackupFile> files = new Collection<BackupFile>();
    private IReadOnlyList<MixERPGridViewColumn> columns;

    /// <summary>
    ///     MixERPUserControlBase.OnControlLoad is abstract, so every ported control declares it.
    ///     The legacy body called CreateHeader / CreateFormSegment / CreateGridModal - all
    ///     markup now - and the two non-markup parts it reached (the default backup name, which
    ///     needs the login, and BindGridView(), which needs the catalog-derived server model)
    ///     are asynchronous and live in OnInitializedAsync. Nothing was dropped.
    /// </summary>
    public override void OnControlLoad(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        //base.OnInitializedAsync runs BOTH authorization checks - never skip it.
        await base.OnInitializedAsync();

        MixERP.Net.Framework.Security.LoginInfo login = await this.CurrentLogin.GetLoginAsync();
        string catalog = await this.CurrentLogin.GetCatalogAsync();

        //The readonly field of the original, built here because DatabaseName needs the catalog.
        PostgreSQLServer server = GetServer(catalog);

        this.columns = GetColumns();
        this.backupName = GetDefaultBackupName(server, login);
        this.BindGridView(server);
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        await base.OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender);

        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        //The controller reads #BackupButton, #Messages, .ui.celled.list, .ui.blue.header and
        //#BackupNameInputText out of the DOM at load time, so it runs after the first render.
        await ModuleScripts.LoadAsync(this.JS, ControllerScript);
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     The <c>readonly PostgreSQLServer server</c> field initializer of the original, with
    ///     <c>AppUsers.GetCurrentUserDB()</c> replaced by the circuit-safe catalog. Every other
    ///     value still comes from <c>Resource/Configs/DbServer.config</c> through
    ///     <c>ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter</c>.
    /// </summary>
    private static PostgreSQLServer GetServer(string catalog)
    {
        return new PostgreSQLServer
        {
            BinDirectory = ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("PostgreSQLBinDirectory"),
            DatabaseBackupDirectory = ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("DatabaseBackupDirectory"),
            DatabaseName = catalog,
            HostName = ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("Server"),
            PortNumber = Conversion.TryCastInteger(ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("Port")),
            UserId = ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("UserId"),
            Password = ConfigurationHelper.GetDbServerParameter("Password")
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Port of <c>CreateBackupNameField</c>'s value: <c>server.Validate()</c> and then the
    ///     name ONLY when <c>server.IsValid</c>, in the invariant culture. An invalid server
    ///     leaves the box EMPTY, which is the original behaviour and the screen's own
    ///     "misconfigured" signal.
    /// </summary>
    private static string GetDefaultBackupName(PostgreSQLServer server,
        MixERP.Net.Framework.Security.LoginInfo login)
    {
        server.Validate();

        if (!server.IsValid)
        {
            return string.Empty;
        }

        return string.Format(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, "{0}.{1}.{2}.{3}",
            server.DatabaseName, login.OfficeId, login.UserName, DateTime.Now.ToFileTime());
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Port of <c>BindGridView()</c>, step for step: the mapped backup directory, the
    ///     <c>Directory.Exists</c> guard, the <c>*.zip</c> enumeration, the anchor-in-the-Name
    ///     trick and the three timestamps.
    /// </summary>
    private void BindGridView(PostgreSQLServer server)
    {
        //Was System.Web.Hosting.HostingEnvironment.MapPath(server.DatabaseBackupDirectory).
        string backupDirectory = MixERPConfiguration.MapPath(server.DatabaseBackupDirectory);

        if (backupDirectory == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (!Directory.Exists(backupDirectory))
        {
            return;
        }

        DirectoryInfo directory = new DirectoryInfo(backupDirectory);
        Collection<BackupFile> files = new Collection<BackupFile>();

        foreach (FileInfo fileInfo in directory.GetFiles("*.zip"))
        {
            if (fileInfo != null)
            {
                BackupFile file = new BackupFile();

                //Was this.ResolveUrl(...) - the Control.ResolveUrl of a WebForms control.
                string downloadPath =
                    PageUtility.ResolveUrl(Path.Combine(server.DatabaseBackupDirectory, fileInfo.Name));

                //The Name carries MARKUP, which is why the grid runs with HtmlEncode="false".
                file.Name = string.Format(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                    "<a href='{0}' target='_blank'>{1}</a>", downloadPath, fileInfo.Name);
                file.CreatedOn = fileInfo.CreationTime;
                file.LastWrittenOn = fileInfo.LastWriteTime;
                file.LastAccessedOn = fileInfo.LastAccessTime;

                files.Add(file);
            }
        }

        this.files = files;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Port of <c>Grid_RowDataBound</c>'s header branch, which localized EVERY header
    ///     (<c>0 .. Count</c>), combined with the column set a GridView auto-generated from
    ///     <see cref="BackupFile" /> - i.e. the PROPERTY DECLARATION ORDER, which is what the
    ///     user saw.
    /// </summary>
    private static IReadOnlyList<MixERPGridViewColumn> GetColumns()
    {
        return new List<MixERPGridViewColumn>
        {
            new MixERPGridViewColumn("CreatedOn", Titles.Get("CreatedOn"), typeof(DateTime)),
            new MixERPGridViewColumn("LastAccessedOn", Titles.Get("LastAccessedOn"), typeof(DateTime)),
            new MixERPGridViewColumn("LastWrittenOn", Titles.Get("LastWrittenOn"), typeof(DateTime)),
            new MixERPGridViewColumn("Name", Titles.Get("Name"), typeof(string))
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     The original's nested <c>internal class BackupFile</c>, unchanged - including the
    ///     PROPERTY ORDER, which decided the grid's column order.
    /// </summary>
    internal class BackupFile
    {
        public DateTime CreatedOn { get; set; }
        public DateTime LastAccessedOn { get; set; }
        public DateTime LastWrittenOn { get; set; }
        public string Name { get; set; }
    }
}
